00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.460
As a bit of background,
Kaggle is the world's largest

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.760
online community of data
scientists, machine learning

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.820
engineers, and AI engineers.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.860
And we have about
2.8 million users

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.610
who come to Kaggle for
three main motivations.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.580
First, to learn and improve
their skills working with data

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.640
and starting to work on machine
learning and AI problems

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.660
through a mix of learning
from content that we create,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.110
content that our users
publish, as well as

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.290
competitions that we put up
that provide great test beds

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.270
for sharpening your skills.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:32.080
Secondly, to compete
in the machine learning

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.410
competitions that we
host and demonstrate

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.420
how your methodologies
and techniques work

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.670
in a fair playing
ground to evaluate them

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:42.890
compared to thousands of
other users around the world.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.970
And then, finally, to
collaborate on data and AI

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:51.220
projects as an individual
or as part of small teams

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.900
as you work on sharing
and collaborating

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.450
on code to analyze data.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.100
And we started hosting
AI competitions in 2010.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.810
And for most of
our history, we've

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.470
been really focused on hosting
these competitions where

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.260
companies, nonprofits and
research groups would come

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.120
to us with a large
number of machine

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.910
learning challenges
alongside datasets to address

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.062
these challenges.

00:03:17.062 --> 00:03:18.520
And then we'd
collaborate with them

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.680
to define well-structured
AI problems on those assets

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.135
and then post them to our
community, who would then

00:03:24.135 --> 00:03:25.510
participate in
these competitions

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.900
and develop solutions
on these datasets.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.530
And that provided
a really great way

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:34.450
to gain insights into how
machine learning was evolving

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.768
as well as frictions
that users hit

00:03:36.768 --> 00:03:38.560
as they're trying to
apply machine learning

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.970
to real problems.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:42.850
And based on those pain points
and insights that we saw,

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:46.510
we started to expand our
platform into our community

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.930
beyond competitions in
2015 with the launch

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:52.240
of our reproducible research
platform in a public data

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.540
platform, as well as in 2018
the launch of our learning

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.710
resource.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:59.770
So I'm going to dive deeper into
each of these parts of Kaggle.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.420
And as I do this,
I encourage you

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.580
to think about how you
can leverage our platform

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.120
to accelerate your own
data and machine learning

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.640
work professionally.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.470
So first as an introduction,
what is a Kaggle competition?

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:16.390
We run dozens of
public competitions

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:20.079
simultaneously, many with large
amounts of prize money attached

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:20.920
to them.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.340
And each of these competitions
has a very well-defined AI

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.150
problem.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:27.760
I will just walk you through
a simple example first.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.430
This is on applying
regression techniques

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.430
as a Getting Started
tutorial competition

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:36.810
to predict the house prices.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.640
And we'll start with a dataset
from a competition host

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.018
with a target variable on it.

00:04:41.018 --> 00:04:43.060
So in this case, we're
trying to predict the sale

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.400
price of a home based on
a small number of things,

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.650
like the size of the
home, whether it's

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.140
a home or condominium, the
size of the lot it's on,

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.760
things like that.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.160
We will take the dataset
from the competition host

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.200
and figure out a good
way to split into a train

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.940
set and a test set.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.250
We will then release
the train set

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.400
and all but the solution on the
test set to our community, who

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.950
will then train models
on the training set

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:09.640
and make predictions on the
test set and then submit

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.040
their predictions back to us.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.860
We will compare their
predictions to the ground truth

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.650
and then calculate scores
based on those predictions

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:21.040
on a public and
private leaderboard.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.810
Then the really
important part of this

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.970
is participants see
real-time feedback

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:26.620
on how they're doing
in the competition

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.403
on the public leaderboard,
both their own score

00:05:29.403 --> 00:05:30.820
and performance
in the competition

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:35.500
as well as how this compares to
every other participant in it.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.330
And this creates
the dynamic that

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.300
really drives the progress
of the competition forward,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.430
because it's the difference
between a user making

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.050
one or two passes
on the problem,

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.420
feeling pretty good about
themselves or pretty bad

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:47.740
about themselves and giving up.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:52.580
And saying like, oh wow, how
did this person get ahead of me?

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.000
And then wanting to try
improving their results

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.270
to match or beat the performance
of the people in front of them.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:03.220
And over time, this dynamic
drives the competition

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.280
to extract nearly all the
usable signal from the dataset,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.970
potentially limited by the
inherent noise in the problem,

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.670
as well as any limitations in
the current state of the art

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:14.332
in the machine learning.

00:06:14.332 --> 00:06:15.790
So what this graph
is going to show

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:20.230
is it's going to graph the
competition progress over time

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:24.710
and the top score on the
public leaderboard over time.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.130
So what typically
happens is pretty quickly

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.410
we get a submission that's
a decent improvement

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:32.350
over the benchmark
in the competition.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.330
And then that
submission is being

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:36.140
advised by another
submission that is also

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.830
a pretty large improvement.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.450
In this dynamic, leapfrogging
occurs a couple times

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.940
early in the competition
until we hit a point

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:45.370
where it's very,
very tough to improve

00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:47.302
their results beyond that.

00:06:47.302 --> 00:06:49.510
Even with an increasing
number of teams participating

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:51.490
and people trying to
tackle this problem,

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.340
we don't see a large
increase in performance.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.590
And this is a really
promising sign

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.060
that we've hit the
limit of what is

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:02.260
achievable on this dataset
given the current limitations

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:05.230
of the state of the art, as
well as any inherent noise

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.000
in the dataset.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.220
And this is a sign,
if you're managing

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:10.780
a team of machine
learning engineers

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:12.773
as well, that you don't
want to invest more

00:07:12.773 --> 00:07:15.440
in trying to improve the machine
learning piece of this problem.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.320
Maybe if you're trying to
improve your performance

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.695
beyond that, you should be
collecting a lot more training

00:07:19.695 --> 00:07:22.300
data or you should be
doing some other aspect

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.937
of the problem that isn't
based on the current dataset

00:07:24.937 --> 00:07:26.020
that we have to work with.

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:31.780
Then another kind of really
unique aspect of competitions

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.320
is, even though there's a
competitive dynamic driving

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.070
them, most participants
in the competition

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:37.540
are not trying to win.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:39.790
They're coming to learn
and improve their skills.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:43.390
And this results in a highly,
highly collaborative, helpful,

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.580
and detail-oriented
competition forums

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:48.070
where people are sharing
what techniques work well

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:52.090
on the problem as well as
insights that they've learned.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:55.210
As well as if they get
stuck, helping other users

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.370
get unstuck.

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:59.050
And this is just a quick
example of some forum post

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:02.260
that you'll see the really
practical detailed tips

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:03.760
required to do
well on the problem

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:06.280
versus broad statements.

00:08:08.385 --> 00:08:10.010
And then at the end
of the competition,

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.740
final results are unveiled
on a private leaderboard

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:17.750
for both prize money
as well as recognition.

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:22.190
And to take a step back,
probably the most famous

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:23.810
machine learning
algorithm in the US

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:28.650
is the Zillow's estimate score
for estimating house prices.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.430
And we collaborated with
Zillow at a competition

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:35.809
recently that involved
predicting future house price

00:08:35.809 --> 00:08:37.309
sales and out-of-time samples.

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:40.960
And this actually improved
on the Zestimate by 13%.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.370
So this provides a
really concrete example

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:46.202
of how this dynamic can help
improve real-world machine

00:08:46.202 --> 00:08:46.910
learning results.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:53.300
If we look at user
profiles on Kaggle,

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:56.360
a user's profile will highlight
their best Kaggle results

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:58.700
and really showcase
here's how well

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.510
they can perform on applied
machine learning problems.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:06.080
And can be a critical part
to helping many of our users

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:10.950
find jobs, and transition
into the field, and get hired.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.980
And alongside this, we
maintain a global ranking

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.310
of all top Kaggle
competitors over time.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:22.100
The next question
I wanted to tackle

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:25.600
is, what use cases do our
customers tackle with Kaggle AI

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:26.540
competitions?

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.670
And my goal is to
give a broad overview

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:30.680
now of the different use
cases that we tackle.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:32.780
This isn't going to
cover everything,

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.780
but it's going to be a
representative sample.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:38.330
The first set
that's become really

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.480
prominent in recent years
based on the combination

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:43.760
of the success of
deep learning as well

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.760
as widely available
image datasets and cost

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.310
effective human
labeling has fostered

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:52.280
a large growth in computer
vision competitions.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:54.680
And these range from
image classification,

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.800
object boundary detection,
and object recognition

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:01.580
to environmental problems
like detect facial recognition

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:04.190
in whales or counting
how many sea lions

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.740
that you see in images there.

00:10:09.233 --> 00:10:11.630
A subset of this category
that I'm personally

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:16.550
really excited about is
using automated methodologies

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:19.340
to improve medical
imaging results

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.830
and start to tackle the
challenge that we only

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:25.220
have a very small number
of trained physicians

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:29.630
in the world, relative to the
size of the global population,

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:32.000
who can read these images.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.160
And that means
that we're missing

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:37.460
results of a huge fraction
of the population.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:39.530
One example of that
was we collaborated

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:42.740
with a company called MobileODT
that's developing a device that

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:48.140
can be attached to a cell phone
for cervical cancer imaging

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:52.370
and then can flag, if it's
for patients in rural Africa,

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:55.983
if they should be seeing a
doctor in a followup visit

00:10:55.983 --> 00:10:58.400
and going to an urban city to
see a doctor and a follow-up

00:10:58.400 --> 00:10:59.120
visit on that.

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:04.610
A third broad category, moving
back to the online world,

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.250
is around recommender systems
and personalized recommender

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:11.330
systems where these have been
very effective at improving

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.460
results ranging from
consumer products

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.210
to things that are directly
useful for machine learning

00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:17.960
engineers as well,
like recommending which

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:21.120
of our packages you should be
using in your day-to-day work.

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.900
Another distinct
and more challenging

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:29.410
stat is forecasting
product demand over time

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:32.170
on out-of-time samples,
where you can no longer make

00:11:32.170 --> 00:11:35.470
the assumption that past
performance or past results are

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:38.680
going to be drawn from
the same distribution

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:39.890
of the future results.

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:43.300
And this introduces a
really complex time element

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.840
into the machine learning
models in the forecasts that

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.615
need to be done for
tackling these challenges.

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:56.520
Another broad category that is
a really important challenge

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:58.620
to solve is
improving our ability

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:02.980
to apply automated methods for
text and speech understanding,

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:05.370
especially with a large
increase in online content

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.665
recently into everything
that that entails.

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:13.853
And then, finally, looking at
some more industrial use cases,

00:12:13.853 --> 00:12:15.270
we've started to
see an increasing

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:18.420
number of predictive
maintenance use cases

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:23.280
for predicting which parts will
require maintenance over time

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.620
in advance of the part failing
and the maintenance being

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:26.120
required.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:32.770
So, next, I want to
look at what insights

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:34.870
have we learned from
seeing how users

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:37.970
approach these competitions.

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:41.720
And the first is probably
everyone's favorite topic

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:43.700
when they look at
what works well

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:47.120
in machine learning, which is
what tools and techniques work

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:48.440
well?

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:50.270
Over the past
several years, that

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.920
has a pat, a really
pithy summary.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:54.980
And there's tooled
types of techniques

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:57.080
that have been
omnipresent in winning

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.420
a lot of different types
and diverse types of Kaggle

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:00.650
competitions.

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:03.800
The first are flavors of
ensemble decision trees,

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:07.100
like radium-boosted
machines with XGBoost

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.710
is one of the really common
successful high-quality

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.370
implementations of that.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:13.670
And then secondly--
deep neural networks

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:16.820
for virtually-structured
natural input,

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.650
like computer vision
data, with Keras

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:24.410
as being one example of a really
popular library implementing

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:25.000
that.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:27.950
And we actually
recently surveyed

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:31.290
Kaggle winners since 2016 for
which primary ML framework

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:33.170
were they using.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:35.420
And then you see
this, coming out here,

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:39.157
where we have three deep
learning frameworks that

00:13:39.157 --> 00:13:41.240
stood out as the primary
framework the winners had

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:41.740
used--

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:44.480
Keras, PyTorch, and TensorFlow.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.090
And then two--
ensemble decision tree

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:49.520
gradient boosting machine
frameworks, LightGBM

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.330
and XGBoost.

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:56.510
And these are so widely used
both because of their ease

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:59.150
of use and ease of getting
started on challenges

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:02.210
as well as the ability to go
really deep and iterate quickly

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:04.580
and expose the complexity
when you need it

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:07.430
in improving the machine
learning algorithms

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:11.510
that you're using to tackle
the specific problem.

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:14.360
We also added a secondary
question to that survey--

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:18.050
for what auxiliary ML software
tools are you also using

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:19.670
to compete in competitions?

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:21.470
And the one that
really stood out here

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:25.100
is, SciKit Learn was rarely
used as the primary tool

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:26.180
by the winners.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.730
However, the feature
pre-processing and the feature

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:30.500
selection and
ensembling techniques

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:35.087
that were present in it were a
highly helpful auxiliary tool

00:14:35.087 --> 00:14:36.170
for our different winners.

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:41.090
From a process
standpoint, we've also

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:45.960
had one clear lesson that
has stood out from hundreds

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:47.880
of winner's interviews.

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:51.240
And that is-- the
people who do well

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:54.960
at applying machine learning
do not take a complex idea,

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.780
then try that one
complex idea over

00:14:56.780 --> 00:14:59.370
a three-month to
12-month time period.

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:02.532
Because a lot of ideas that
we start off with are bad.

00:15:02.532 --> 00:15:04.740
Instead, what they do is
they optimize their workflow

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.470
for learning and experimenting
through a lot of bad ideas

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:10.210
to find the ones that actually
work or start working well,

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:13.390
and then really honing in
on the ideas that work well.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:15.150
So instead of
trying to implement

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:19.200
one large and complex
idea, they instead

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:24.670
try many ideas over time,
implement them quickly,

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:26.832
evaluate how they work,
analyze their results

00:15:26.832 --> 00:15:29.040
and what they learned, and
then go through this cycle

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:31.500
again and again and again.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.080
And the teams that
perform well are the teams

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:38.520
that are effective at both going
through the cycle many times

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.350
as well as paying
close attention to what

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:44.160
they were learning from
going through the cycle.

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:46.060
And in a similar
vein, this iteration

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:48.880
is not just done on the
single layer machine

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:50.500
learning component.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.410
Instead, for any
applied AI model,

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:55.630
you go from an interim component
from a business problem

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:56.680
to a production system.

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:59.230
That includes finding
source datasets to use.

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.150
How do you split
the data in order

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:03.220
to do your internal
evaluation, as well as

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:06.790
your final evaluation samples?

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:08.430
Which evaluation
metrics do you use?

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:10.453
How do you extract features
and select features?

00:16:10.453 --> 00:16:11.620
How do you train your model?

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:13.220
How do you do model selection?

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:14.595
And finally, how
do you implement

00:16:14.595 --> 00:16:16.300
that in a production system?

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:18.665
Our competitions don't
currently tackle the first piece

00:16:18.665 --> 00:16:20.540
in the last piece of
that in the darker blue.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.960
But some of our competitions
tackle the interim pieces

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:25.540
in between those.

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:29.500
And it's critical to optimize
those entire workflows, not

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:31.840
just that machine learning
piece in the middle there.

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:38.580
And given this rapid
iteration and a large number

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.070
of experiments that
our users are running,

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:44.070
another key piece that
successful participants do

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:47.310
is they keep very careful
track of all their experimental

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:49.995
results for their learning, as
well as using version controls

00:16:49.995 --> 00:16:52.680
so they can scroll
back at a point in time

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.710
when they need to go back to
an idea that had some promise,

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:58.170
and pull that back up, and
really understand what worked

00:16:58.170 --> 00:16:59.420
about it and what drove that.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:06.430
Another piece-- and this
has been true for almost all

00:17:06.430 --> 00:17:09.490
of the several hundred general
competitions that we've run--

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:13.450
is that they've been won by
large ensembles of models

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:18.400
versus a single model.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.400
And these large ensembles
almost always eke out

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:22.940
very marginal additional gain.

00:17:22.940 --> 00:17:25.480
So it's like, the single
best model in there

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:30.020
might be 99.5% of
the best performance.

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:32.320
Then that additional 0.5%
performance improvement

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:35.560
comes from taking a large number
of different complex models

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:38.500
and combining them
all together, which

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:40.390
is great for squeezing
out the last bit

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:41.500
of predictive accuracy.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:43.660
But putting my software
engineering hat on,

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:46.540
it's about the last thing I'd
want to put into production.

00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:49.990
So instead, we'll focus on that
single best performing model

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:51.630
within that ensemble.

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:53.380
That is the one that
might make more sense

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:54.708
to put in production.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:17:59.650
Another lesson
that we've learned

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:02.380
is, successful
participants very analyze

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:04.960
how does their model
generalize in new data points

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:07.930
and try avoiding overfitting.

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:09.430
One of our users
learned this lesson

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:11.352
the hard way when he
watched himself fall off

00:18:11.352 --> 00:18:13.810
the leaderboard in the final
minute of the competition when

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:15.850
it transitioned from
the public leaderboard

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:18.453
to the holdout set in
the private leaderboard.

00:18:18.453 --> 00:18:19.870
And I don't think
he's ever failed

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:21.070
a competition since then.

00:18:25.092 --> 00:18:26.550
So I want to talk
a little bit more

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:32.040
about where we are going
with Kaggle AI competitions.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:34.050
Almost every competition
that we've run so far

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:35.550
has been of the form--

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:37.890
we have a training dataset
and a test dataset.

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:39.690
Users download the
training dataset,

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:42.572
make predictions on the trainer
model and the training dataset,

00:18:42.572 --> 00:18:44.030
make predictions
on the test asset,

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:47.070
and upload their
predictions to Kaggle.

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:50.208
Uploading predictions is
limiting in a number of ways.

00:18:50.208 --> 00:18:52.500
So we're starting to expand
the competitions that we're

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:55.260
hosting beyond
uploading predictions

00:18:55.260 --> 00:19:00.900
and to uploading code and
using the code as a submission.

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:03.510
So the way this works for a
small number of competitions

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:06.270
now is users will write
the code to train the model

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:10.230
and make predictions of it,
through a code execution

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:15.465
service that we run and then
submit that code to Kaggle.

00:19:15.465 --> 00:19:17.713
A couple examples
of this where it's

00:19:17.713 --> 00:19:20.130
been really helpful to enable
us to run these competitions

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.820
in a high-quality
way is two of them

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:25.980
have touched on back
testing and forecasting

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.320
time series in a
financial modeling problem

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:30.420
where, for any time
series challenge,

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:33.223
you don't want to have a single
trained asset and test asset.

00:19:33.223 --> 00:19:34.890
And instead, you want
to stream the data

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:37.600
into the model over time and
make predictions going forward

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:40.020
at each time increment.

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:42.780
It's also helped us run more
effective competitions around

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:45.810
challenges where a human
could go through and label

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:48.210
the answers to the results,
because we can now run

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:50.010
competitions on
hidden test assets,

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:52.250
where a human can actually
go through in hand-label

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:54.810
the results of the test set--

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:57.750
like for text or
image competitions.

00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:00.510
And then finally, it's let us
experiment with competitions

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:01.900
around adversarial learning.

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:05.110
We have a really fun
cat-and-mouse dynamic where--

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:08.520
let's say that I have
a picture of a cat

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.380
and I want to
trick my classifier

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.900
into thinking this is a
picture of a dog, not a cat,

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:17.940
by making small, imperceptible
modifications to that picture.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:19.890
And then we ran a
series of competitions

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:24.420
around predicting models
that modify images

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:26.610
to trick classifiers,
as well as models

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:28.270
that should classify
those images

00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:30.270
and be robust against
those adversarial attacks.

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:35.190
And in the future,
this will unlock

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:38.370
a bunch of exciting new
flavors of AI competitions,

00:20:38.370 --> 00:20:42.600
ranging from backtesting time
series and live data feeds

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.670
to reinforcement
learning challenges,

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.410
where instead of a
machine learning model

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:48.900
making predictions, you have
an agent that's taking actions

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:50.400
and interacting
with an environment.

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:53.025
And it's scored based on how it
interacts with that environment

00:20:53.025 --> 00:20:54.180
over time.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:56.340
As well as generative
modeling competitions,

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:00.390
where you are creating new
images or new tech samples

00:21:00.390 --> 00:21:02.610
and evaluating
whether or not those

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:06.940
images or text samples mirror
the real distributions.

00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:09.660
Adversarial learning
competitions--

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:12.090
that's another one
that I mentioned.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:14.910
Another area that's become
increasingly important,

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:16.770
as machine learning
has moved out

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:19.232
of research and into
many applications

00:21:19.232 --> 00:21:20.940
throughout the world,
is machine learning

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:22.890
under computational constraints.

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:26.850
Because if you're implementing
machine learning at scale,

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:30.420
it is very costly to do the
machine learning predictions,

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:33.480
both in terms of dollars
and energy usage,

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:35.250
as well as there's
a lot of scenarios

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:37.710
where you want a very low
latency and low energy

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:38.350
predictions.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.770
For example, I could have
the best speech recognition

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:41.970
model in the world.

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:45.900
But if I'm in a low-connectivity
scenario with my cell phone,

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:49.200
that model is useless to me
if it can't [? broaden ?]

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:52.040
quickly on my phone.

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:55.180
And then finally, it will let
us run competitions on sensitive

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:57.670
datasets where we cannot
release the dataset publicly

00:21:57.670 --> 00:22:00.760
but, instead, we can bring
the code and the models

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:02.633
to the datasets privately.

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:09.240
Now, as I mentioned before, we
spent most of the past decade

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:13.020
or so focused on building
and expanding AI competitions

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:14.940
in the community around that.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:19.850
But more recently, we've been
building beyond AI competitions

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:20.350
as well.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:22.642
And I wanted to talk a little
bit about our motivations

00:22:22.642 --> 00:22:23.850
for that.

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:26.820
So the global community that
we built around competitions

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:30.120
has provided a lot of
insight for current frictions

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:33.240
on applying AI through
our experience working

00:22:33.240 --> 00:22:37.660
with hundreds of hosts and
organizations around the world

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:40.290
and seen their frictions
that they structured

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:42.570
in creating machine
learning problems

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:44.310
and deploying the
datasets for those,

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:46.260
from our experience in
working with our users

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:48.488
and seeing how they
started tackling problems

00:22:48.488 --> 00:22:50.280
and all the frictions
they had doing things

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:52.050
like sharing code
with each other,

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:55.190
as well as from our
own internal work.

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:57.690
And I'll summarize
some of the ones that

00:22:57.690 --> 00:22:59.670
have served as inspiration
for me and for us,

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:02.820
as we start to expand
beyond competitions.

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:05.070
The first is that
we've hit many barriers

00:23:05.070 --> 00:23:07.470
to accessing data,
which still lives

00:23:07.470 --> 00:23:10.260
in silos within
different organizations

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:14.160
and many different forms.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:18.272
And then, once we do
have access to that data,

00:23:18.272 --> 00:23:19.980
understanding the
context behind the data

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:21.480
makes you feel like a detective.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.270
And getting that data
into a high-quality state

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:26.370
can be really tough.

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:30.670
For a couple examples that we've
hit, trying to clean data--

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:34.560
one of them is, I was working
with a dataset of flight

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:37.500
arrival and departure
times for training models

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:39.930
to predict how long
a flight will take.

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:41.580
And then on the
first pass of it,

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.950
I was surprised to see that
I had a bunch of flights

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.330
that had negative duration.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:46.890
Then I was thinking, oh, OK.

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:50.670
I must have messed up the time
zone processing in my code

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:52.290
or something like that.

00:23:52.290 --> 00:23:53.700
I look at that-- no.

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:56.932
Time zones were right,
including for flights taking off

00:23:56.932 --> 00:23:58.140
and landing in the time zone.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:00.840
For whatever reason, there's
just a couple percentage

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:04.080
of these flights that were
put in incorrectly as actually

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:10.170
landing before they took
off, which made no sense.

00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:12.630
And then, if I'm
trying to make a model

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:15.472
to predict flight
durations, then I naively

00:24:15.472 --> 00:24:17.430
throw a bunch of flights
with negative duration

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:21.180
in there, that'll throw
my entire results way off.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:22.140
As another example--

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:25.320
I was working with a
dataset of bulldozer prices.

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:27.810
And one of the fields
as an input into that

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:30.290
was the year the bulldozer
was manufactured.

00:24:30.290 --> 00:24:32.700
I was flipping through
that and see things

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.622
like 1983, 1995, 1972.

00:24:35.622 --> 00:24:37.080
I'm thinking, OK,
this makes sense.

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:38.622
Then I start seeing
a bunch of fields

00:24:38.622 --> 00:24:40.255
that say 1000, 1000, 1000.

00:24:40.255 --> 00:24:43.840
And bulldozers didn't exist
before the steam engine.

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:45.780
It didn't exist
in the year 1000.

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:49.050
It turns out that, somewhere
in the complex set of processes

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:52.410
to create that dataset from
a lot of different sources,

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:54.030
one person decided,
you know what?

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:57.660
1,000 is a great number
to use as a missing value.

00:24:57.660 --> 00:24:59.850
So 1,000 just meant
that they didn't

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:02.910
know when it was manufactured.

00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:05.700
And as a third example--

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:10.490
we were working with a
dataset of student essays

00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:13.645
that we had transcribers
go and digitize those

00:25:13.645 --> 00:25:16.020
with the instructions that--
if you can't read the essay,

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:17.010
please, throw it out.

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:18.840
And don't send us the
data back for that.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.890
And mark it as,
you can't read it.

00:25:20.890 --> 00:25:23.940
Instead, we were starting
to train some baseline

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:27.168
models on this and saw that
there was one essay in there--

00:25:27.168 --> 00:25:29.460
and these were supposed to
be about 1,000-word essays--

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:31.210
that just said, this
essay got good marks.

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:33.350
But as far as I can
tell, it's gibberish

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:35.610
that scored very highly.

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:38.760
And if I'd actually thrown that
into a machine learning model

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:40.860
with a very small
number of datasets

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:42.720
that would have really
thrown my model off.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:44.850
Because there was a case
where that transcriber

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:47.270
didn't follow the instructions.

00:25:47.270 --> 00:25:49.410
Then pretty much
any data problem

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:52.740
like this has a large number
of examples where you really

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:54.720
need to dig deep into
that asset to understand

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:58.082
the context behind it's created
and what errors may exist

00:25:58.082 --> 00:26:00.540
with it, to successfully apply
machine learning models that

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:03.780
work well on it.

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:06.510
And another flavor of
that is understanding

00:26:06.510 --> 00:26:10.050
which forms of leakage
may emerge near the asset.

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:12.240
It's a really common
and subtle problem

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:15.030
for a lot of machine
learning applications.

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:18.510
Leakage is where you're
trying to use information

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:21.960
to make predictions
on the new samples

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.170
that you don't actually have at
the time of making predictions.

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:28.020
That new sample source is
invalid to use for predictions

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.410
and new samples.

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.980
And one example of this
is, we got a dataset

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:37.258
a while ago on prostate
cancer prediction

00:26:37.258 --> 00:26:39.300
and trying to predict the
likelihood of a patient

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:41.360
to have prostate cancer.

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:43.500
It had a couple
hundred fields in there

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:45.303
with [INAUDIBLE]
obscurely named that we

00:26:45.303 --> 00:26:46.470
didn't know what they meant.

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:48.180
So we trained a
baseline model on it,

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:52.530
then looked at what was
driving the results.

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:54.720
And it turns out, this
innocuously named feature,

00:26:54.720 --> 00:26:56.730
prossurg, was
buried in the middle

00:26:56.730 --> 00:26:59.940
of that that represented--
this patient had had

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:01.535
surgery for prostate cancer.

00:27:01.535 --> 00:27:03.660
So it's highly, highly
predictive of whether or not

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:06.300
the patient was likely
to have prostate cancer,

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:09.340
but was entirely useless in
applying in any new patient

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:09.840
scenario.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:19.250
Then another kind of common
pain point that we've seen is--

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:23.790
reproducing others' work
can take a very long time,

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:27.350
whether I'm going back to work
that I did four years ago,

00:27:27.350 --> 00:27:30.980
I'm trying to take work that
my colleague did and run it

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:34.910
on my machine, or--

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:36.920
even worse-- starting
with a rated paper that's

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:38.820
published online that
I'm really interested in,

00:27:38.820 --> 00:27:40.653
but I don't have the
code data or maybe even

00:27:40.653 --> 00:27:43.910
the computational equipment
that was used to produce that,

00:27:43.910 --> 00:27:46.800
and trying to reproduce
those results.

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:50.780
So this is probably one of the
largest wastes of human capital

00:27:50.780 --> 00:27:52.520
and resources in our
field and that we

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:54.080
spend a tremendous
amount of time

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:57.980
simply trying to get to the same
results that someone else had,

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:00.170
in working through
packaged conflicts, version

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:05.068
conflicts, different data
sets, and a large set of errors

00:28:05.068 --> 00:28:07.610
there, simply to reproduce work
that someone else had already

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:08.110
did.

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:14.460
That brings us to
the first product

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:19.150
that we launched to try starting
to address some of these pain

00:28:19.150 --> 00:28:19.650
points.

00:28:19.650 --> 00:28:21.330
I see a lot of the
frictions I mentioned

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:23.320
as aspirational long-term goals.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:25.650
And if we can even make
a small dent into those,

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:30.020
then we can create some
real good in the world.

00:28:30.020 --> 00:28:33.510
So first, we've launched a
feature called Kaggle Kernels,

00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:36.220
where you can now click New
Kernel on any competition

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:38.460
or dataset on Kaggle.

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:41.080
And this will enable you to
spin up an interactive script

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:46.160
or notebook session in
Python or R on that dataset

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:48.640
and in a docker container.

00:28:48.640 --> 00:28:52.930
And you, within seconds, will
have a source code editor,

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:56.100
an interactive console, and the
raw data at your fingertips,

00:28:56.100 --> 00:29:00.160
so you don't have to do
things like install packages,

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:03.430
because we have most of the
Python and R open source

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:06.297
package ecosystems
installed in there for you.

00:29:06.297 --> 00:29:08.380
You don't have to worry
about downloading the data

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:11.950
or going into the data
or anything like that.

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:15.140
So it's also easy to moderate
things like your compute usage

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:21.500
and add GPUs or additional
compute if you need to in this.

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:24.570
And after you finish coding
or get to a stopping point,

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:26.760
you can click Commit
and then create

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:29.310
a sharable and reproducible
version of your work

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:31.540
in a clean environment.

00:29:31.540 --> 00:29:34.410
The reason we rerun things
in clean environments is--

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.180
I've seen a lot of
Jupyter notebooks

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:39.750
where it might have
15 cells in it.

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:42.970
But those 15 cells have
been run about 1,000 times.

00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:48.872
And what was in the cell
included a lot of hidden stage

00:29:48.872 --> 00:29:50.580
that was necessary to
reproduce the work,

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:52.965
so it's not actually
reproducible.

00:29:52.965 --> 00:29:54.840
And then from here, you
can share your kernel

00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:56.580
with collaborators
or make it public

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:01.620
and share it with our community.

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:06.420
And then we've had, now, a very
large number of high-quality

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:08.340
kernels shared
publicly on Kaggle--

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:10.890
over about 200,000, now
of which about 50,000

00:30:10.890 --> 00:30:13.260
have at least one upvote
from another user,

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:14.910
as a rough indication for--

00:30:14.910 --> 00:30:16.300
another user found
this valuable.

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:21.180
And this is a higher
quality result.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:22.770
And in practice,
this means we now

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:25.680
have a really rich array
of content for, basically,

00:30:25.680 --> 00:30:28.770
any type of data that
you might be working with

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:31.590
and machine learning problem
or visualization problem

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:33.180
that you might be
trying to tackle.

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:36.750
You can find an example of how
to do that in a public dataset

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:38.310
on Kaggle.

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:41.670
So it's helped provide a
really rich way of tutorials

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:44.920
that you can use to
get started with.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:47.460
And furthermore,
because this is being

00:30:47.460 --> 00:30:50.490
backed by versioned
compute, versioned data,

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:53.260
and versioned code, it's
straightforward to fork

00:30:53.260 --> 00:30:55.470
others' kernels that
they have shared here

00:30:55.470 --> 00:30:58.020
and reproduce the
work that they created

00:30:58.020 --> 00:30:59.730
as well as interact
with their examples

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:02.693
and extend it into your own.

00:31:02.693 --> 00:31:04.110
So I see a kernel
that's combining

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:07.600
the three critical components
for reproducible data science.

00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:09.370
The first is versioned code.

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:10.950
The second is versioned data.

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:13.530
And the third is a versioned
computational environment

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:15.320
through docker containers.

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:19.110
And then things on Kaggle
are mostly reproducible,

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:21.940
in the sense that you can
still do things like not

00:31:21.940 --> 00:31:28.400
set a random seed or, if
you're using parallelism,

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:31.550
a lot of functions that
use parallelism have

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:34.430
a dependency on the time
they take to execute

00:31:34.430 --> 00:31:36.600
and for whatever
results are returned.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:38.270
So it's not reproducible
in that sense,

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:40.350
but it's reproducible in
many of the core senses.

00:31:42.910 --> 00:31:45.610
That brings us to one of
the next questions we face.

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:47.635
Well, we've launched
kernels in competitions.

00:31:47.635 --> 00:31:50.050
But I want to load new data
into that kernel as well.

00:31:50.050 --> 00:31:52.370
How do I do that?

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:56.200
And that led us to
launch Kaggle Datasets

00:31:56.200 --> 00:31:57.893
for two different reasons.

00:31:57.893 --> 00:32:00.310
The first was to provide a
versioned and reproducible data

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:03.280
storage to load
data into kernels.

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:06.370
That was straightforward, to
drag files up and drop them in,

00:32:06.370 --> 00:32:10.030
where they're in a versioned
way for future use.

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:14.830
And then secondly, to
provide an interface

00:32:14.830 --> 00:32:17.380
where it was easy to share
data in a high-quality way

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:18.933
to the rest of the world.

00:32:18.933 --> 00:32:20.350
And over the past
couple of years,

00:32:20.350 --> 00:32:21.830
this has grown
really dramatically.

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:23.740
And about 15,000
datasets have now

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:26.380
been publicly shared on Kaggle.

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:30.190
And my longer-term goal is
to have all the public data

00:32:30.190 --> 00:32:33.220
in the world accessible through
a common interface like this,

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:34.080
so I no longer--

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:35.747
if I'm trying to work
with weather data,

00:32:35.747 --> 00:32:38.410
for example-- have to go
to an FTP server from NOAA

00:32:38.410 --> 00:32:42.130
to try getting that obscure
weather that I'm trying to pull

00:32:42.130 --> 00:32:45.880
in, or I'm trying to dig through
a complex data dictionary

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:49.510
from the census to
understand-- what does

00:32:49.510 --> 00:32:51.760
this field in the census,
as one of the most commonly

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:52.960
used datasets, mean?

00:32:55.940 --> 00:32:58.770
Then all of these datasets
are downloadable and browsable

00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:00.940
in web UI.

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:02.710
And we're really
targeting making

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:06.340
these datasets easy to consume
both for humans and machines.

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:09.610
So the machine piece is
connecting it to Kaggle Kernels

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:12.280
and, for ease of use for
consumption, in Python and R.

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:15.550
And the human piece is providing
clear summaries of what's

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:17.650
in the data for
things like histograms

00:33:17.650 --> 00:33:19.580
on the number of unique
values in a field,

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:21.700
the number of missing
values in the field,

00:33:21.700 --> 00:33:26.320
as well as collaborative human
written descriptions of--

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:27.910
what's does this column mean?

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:28.960
What does this file mean?

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:33.900
What's in this file?-- and
components along those lines.

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:36.990
And these datasets
link all the code

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:39.540
that users have publicly
shared on top of them as well.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:41.730
So if you go and publish
it out on Kaggle,

00:33:41.730 --> 00:33:44.460
you get the advantage of
seeing how that is used

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:47.940
and how other users have
built on your dataset

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:49.477
and created code
on your dataset.

00:33:49.477 --> 00:33:51.810
And then if you're trying to
get started on the dataset,

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:54.240
then you don't just start
with an Excel spreadsheet

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:57.360
or a CSV file or
the file itself.

00:33:57.360 --> 00:33:58.860
Instead, you start with--

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:01.050
here's 1,000 different
code examples

00:34:01.050 --> 00:34:04.320
that other users have written
on it for how to read that data

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:06.870
and how to visualize it,
how to pre-process it,

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:09.250
how to apply machine
learning algorithms to it,

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:10.659
and things along those lines.

00:34:14.739 --> 00:34:18.690
And then, after we'd started
to build up all these resources

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:20.280
and create all this
content in Kaggle,

00:34:20.280 --> 00:34:22.530
we started to get the
question more and more--

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:24.449
I want to get started
with machine learning,

00:34:24.449 --> 00:34:25.960
and I do not know where to go.

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:28.750
What should I do?

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:30.239
And that led us
to start building

00:34:30.239 --> 00:34:32.315
a series of micro-courses
with tutorials

00:34:32.315 --> 00:34:35.550
and interactive exercises
with a goal of providing

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:38.310
a really fast and
straightforward way for users

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:41.820
to get started in the
nearest way possible,

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:44.860
to get them onto their own
projects and their own work.

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:48.130
So these range from
beginner content like Python

00:34:48.130 --> 00:34:51.150
and interest in machine
learning and data visualization.

00:34:51.150 --> 00:34:54.239
And on Pandas, there's a really
nice database manipulation

00:34:54.239 --> 00:34:55.780
library in Python--

00:34:55.780 --> 00:34:59.550
to more advanced
content, like machine

00:34:59.550 --> 00:35:01.900
learning
explainability, a course

00:35:01.900 --> 00:35:08.010
where we want to really
help users understand that--

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:09.810
I'm seeing machine
learning models

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:14.120
as black boxes is a really
common misconception.

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:16.530
And there's a large number
of tools and methodologies

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:19.397
available to you to introspect
a machine learning model

00:35:19.397 --> 00:35:21.480
and to understand what is
driving its performance.

00:35:21.480 --> 00:35:23.550
And why is it making the
predictions that it is.

00:35:27.540 --> 00:35:31.890
So that was a broad overview
of the different pieces

00:35:31.890 --> 00:35:33.640
of Kaggle's platform.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:36.238
I hope that was helpful
for you to understand

00:35:36.238 --> 00:35:38.280
the different components
of what we're working on

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:40.200
and why we're working
on them, as well as

00:35:40.200 --> 00:35:44.550
when you're trying to solve your
next machine learning or data

00:35:44.550 --> 00:35:47.010
problem, hopefully this will
be a helpful resource for you

00:35:47.010 --> 00:35:51.030
to go to and find an example
as a source of inspiration

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:53.220
or see how another
user has tried

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:55.860
to tackle a similar
problem as you,

00:35:55.860 --> 00:35:57.960
and use that as a starting
point versus needing

00:35:57.960 --> 00:36:00.330
to start your work
from a blinking cursor.

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:03.380
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

